[GREEN] detect an installed Playwright browser, fall back to local ARP

Feature 1 — engine detection (`my_lan_prober.browsers`).

`pip install playwright` installs the Python driver and *no* browser; the
binaries are a separate `playwright install`.  The fetcher hard-coded
`playwright.chromium.launch()`, so a machine that only ran
`playwright install firefox` died on a missing executable.

`browsers.py` probes `browser_type.executable_path` — the attribute
Playwright itself launches from — for each of chromium/firefox/webkit and
reports which ones exist.  `first_available_browser` returns the first in
preference order, and `--browser`/`PW_BROWSER` overrides it.  Deriving the
path from the `ms-playwright` cache layout was rejected as a guess at a
private, version-stamped directory name (`chromium-1243`).

Two robustness rules the tests forced, both real failure modes on this
machine rather than hypotheticals:

* `executable_path` is `""` when Playwright has no binary, and
  `Path("")` is the *current directory* — which exists — so the emptiness
  check has to precede the existence check or every engine looks installed;
* the driver is a child process and can die on startup (here: WinError 5
  from `asyncio.windows_utils.pipe()` under the sandbox).  "Cannot tell"
  must read as "not installed" everywhere, so detection degrades to the
  local ARP table instead of raising.

Feature 2 — degrade instead of dying.

`ARPTableFetcher.available()` existed but `FetcherChain` never consulted it,
so an unusable source was still called and still raised.  The chain now
asks first and skips unavailable sources without invoking them, and reports
"every ARP source is unavailable" when none can run.

`--browser` is validated at parse time, so a typo fails with an argparse
error rather than deep inside a browser launch.

Verified against this machine: chromium-1243 is present but the driver
cannot start under the sandbox, and detection correctly answers
`{chromium: False, ...}` with the chain falling through to UnixArpFetcher.
